    "id": "q07",
    "question": "Why should the Hall sensor loss test be run at 9 V?",
    "answer": {
      "found": true,
      "answer": "At 9 V the motor runs slowly, so the Hall pulses are slow. A timer that restarts on noise can then hide the signal loss. The lessons-learned document says the test should be run at both 9 V and 13.5 V, and that sensor-removal tests should use the lowest supply voltage.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "document": "LL-2023-05-hall-connector.md",
          "text": "The 9 V case matters: at low speed the pulses are slow, and a timer that restarts on noise can hide the loss."
        },
        {
          "document": "LL-2023-05-hall-connector.md",
          "text": "Every sensor needs a plausibility check with a timeout, and a test that removes the sensor while the actuator runs, at the lowest supply voltage."
        }
      ]
    },
